     1	# Tessera plain-language style (inspired by ASD-STE100)
     2	
     3	Owner instance, 2026-10-16. The author asked for documentation "written so someone whose native language isn't
     4	Claude can understand it". These rules adapt the main writing rules of ASD-STE100 (Simplified Technical English).
     5	We do not claim STE conformance: we have no approved STE dictionary check. We use the structure rules and our own
     6	dictionary.
     7	
     8	## Rules
     9	
    10	1. One sentence gives one idea.
    11	2. A sentence has a maximum of 20 words if it tells the reader to do something, and 25 words if it describes something.
    12	3. A paragraph has a maximum of 6 sentences and one topic.
    13	4. Use the active voice. Say who or what does the action.
    14	5. Use only the words in the dictionary below for project ideas. Each word has one meaning. Do not use a synonym.
    15	6. Use technical names (for example "DNSSEC", "Bitcoin", "SHA-256") without change. Explain each one when you first use it.
    16	7. Do not use idioms, metaphors or humour.
    17	8. Write steps and conditions as numbered or bulleted lists.
    18	9. Write "must" for a rule and "can" for a possibility. Do not use "should", "may" or "shall".
    19	10. Do not refer to a document section number in the body. Put references at the end.
    20	
    21	## Faithfulness
    22	
    23	A plain document does not replace the signed or governing text. If the two differ, the governing text applies, and
    24	the difference is a finding. Before publication, a reviewer checks each plain document against its source. The
    25	reviewer confirms three things: the plain document contains no new rule, it removes no rule, and it changes no
    26	meaning. The reviewer records each place where the source was unclear or contradictory.
    27	
    28	## Dictionary
    29	
    30	| Word | Meaning | Do not use |
    31	|---|---|---|
    32	| content | The bytes that a customer gives to Tessera. | data, document, payload |
    33	| receipt | The set of files that Tessera gives back for one content. A receipt is the evidence. | attestation, certificate, bundle |
    34	| receipt ID | The SHA-256 hash of the signed core of a receipt. Two copies of one receipt have the same receipt ID. | bundle hash |
    35	| service | The Tessera V1 program that makes receipts. | Tessera (alone), issuer |
    36	| key | A private signing key of the service. V1 has two keys. | signer, slot |
    37	| AWS key | The service key that is in an Amazon (AWS) hardware security module. It uses ML-DSA-65, a post-quantum algorithm. | kms key |
    38	| Google key | The service key that is in a Google Cloud hardware security module. It uses ECDSA P-256. | gpg key, token |
    39	| signature | The result when a key signs bytes. | — |
    40	| window | The period of time in which the two keys are allowed to sign. It has a start and an end. | lifetime, validity |
    41	| issue time | The time that the service writes into the receipt when it signs. A person cannot check this time directly. | declared time |
    42	| anchor time (T) | The time from Bitcoin that proves the receipt existed then. The receipt existed at T or before T. | timestamp |
    43	| authority record | A record that says "these two keys belong to wamason.com and can sign in this window". V1 has two authority records: one in DNS and one in a git repository. | key publication, channel |
    44	| verifier | The program that a person uses to check a receipt. | validator |
    45	| trust file | The file that comes with a verifier. It lists the root keys and the Bitcoin data that the verifier trusts. | trust configuration |
    46	| verdict | The result of a check: VALID, INVALID or CANNOT VERIFY. | status, outcome |
    47	| VALID | All checks passed. | VALID_STRICT (in plain text) |
    48	| INVALID | A check failed. The receipt is wrong or changed. | — |
    49	| CANNOT VERIFY | The verifier does not have what it needs to decide. This is not the same as INVALID. | UNVERIFIABLE (in plain text) |
    50	| attacker | A person who tries to make a false receipt or to change a true receipt. | adversary |
     1	# What Tessera V1 promises (plain version, pilot)
     2	
     3	Status: PILOT, owner instance, 2026-10-16. This is a plain rendering of the opening of Amendment 9 and its claims C1
     4	to C7. Amendment 9 is the signed text, and it governs. Words in this document have the meanings in
     5	`docs/plain/STE-STYLE.md`. A faithfulness review must occur before anyone relies on this text.
     6	
     7	## 1. What V1 does
     8	
     9	Tessera V1 is a service that makes receipts for wamason.com.
    10	
    11	A receipt proves four things:
    12	
    13	1. The service received this exact content.
    14	2. Both keys of the service signed it.
    15	3. Two authority records said that these keys belong to wamason.com, for a stated window.
    16	4. The content and both signatures existed at the anchor time T or before T. Bitcoin fixes T.
    17	
    18	A person can check a receipt without Tessera and without the internet. The person needs only the receipt, a
    19	verifier and its trust file.
    20	
    21	The receipt also checks the window. The issue time must be at the start of the window or after it. The anchor
    22	time T must be at the end of the window or before it.
    23	
    24	## 2. What V1 protects against
    25	
    26	V1 gives the correct verdict when an attacker does one of these things:
    27	
    28	- The attacker changes a receipt, removes a part of it, or replaces a part of it.
    29	- The attacker makes a false receipt.
    30	- The attacker uses a true receipt again for different content, or puts a false date on it.
    31	- The attacker controls the DNS account or the git repository, but cannot sign with the service keys.
    32	- The attacker steals a key after Bitcoin anchored the receipt.
    33	- The attacker breaks a signature algorithm after Bitcoin anchored the receipt.
    34	
    35	These protections are true only if the cryptography, the key rules and Bitcoin work as expected.
    36	
    37	## 3. What V1 does not do
    38	
    39	V1 does not tell you these things:
    40	
    41	- who sent the content;
    42	- what the content means, or if it is true;
    43	- if a key was revoked;
    44	- if a key or algorithm was still safe at the time of signing.
    45	
    46	V1 does not protect against these attackers:
    47	
    48	- An attacker who can sign with both keys during the window. A person who controls the AWS account can do this.
    49	- An attacker who controls all of the author's accounts at the same time.
    50	- An attacker who breaks SHA-256, OpenTimestamps or more than six Bitcoin blocks.
    51	- An attacker who replaces the verifier or its trust file.
    52	
    53	One person controls both keys. The two keys are in different technical systems, but not under different control.
    54	
    55	## 4. The seven claims
    56	
    57	**C1. The bytes.** The verifier finds the exact signed parts of the receipt. Both keys signed the same content.
    58	The receipt ID has only one possible meaning.
    59	*Not claimed:* anything about the meaning or truth of the content.
    60	
    61	**C2. Presentation.** The service received the content at some time.
    62	*Not claimed:* who sent it. *Not claimed:* that a second use of the receipt is correct for a new transaction.
    63	
    64	**C3. The signatures.** Each signature is correct for its signed part. If a signature is missing, removed or
    65	replaced, the receipt is INVALID.
    66	*Not claimed:* that two people must agree before the service signs. One person controls both keys. A person who
    67	controls the AWS account can make both keys sign.
    68	
    69	**C4. Authority.** Two authority records said that the keys belong to wamason.com, for the window. One record is in
    70	DNS, which DNSSEC signs. The other record is in a git repository, which the repository key signs. Bitcoin anchored the
    71	git record at least six blocks before it anchored the receipt.
    72	*Not claimed:* revocation checks. *Not claimed:* that the git record was public. *Not claimed:* an order in clock
    73	time. The order is in Bitcoin blocks.
    74	
    75	**C5. Time.** The signed parts and their exact signatures existed at time T or before T. T comes from the
    76	Bitcoin attestation with the lowest block number in the receipt. Six blocks confirm it.
    77	*Not claimed:* an earliest possible time. *Not claimed:* that this receipt came before a different receipt. A Bitcoin
    78	block time is close to the real time, but it is not exact.
    79	
    80	**C6. Without Tessera.** The verdict depends only on the receipt, the verifier and its trust file.
    81	*Not claimed:* that the holder keeps the receipt. That is the holder's task. If T is after the last Bitcoin block in
    82	the trust file, the verdict is CANNOT VERIFY.
    83	
    84	**C7. A safe decision.** The verdict has a type. The verdict also says that V1 did not check revocation or the
    85	historical safety of the keys. A person who is not an expert must not accept a bad receipt as good. The relying-party
    86	test on 4 December 2026 checks this. That test has not occurred yet.
    87	
    88	## References
    89	
    90	Amendment 9: opening sections "The claim", "The adversary", "The boundaries"; table A9.1 rows C1 to C7.
    91	
    92	## Notes for the faithfulness review (owner)
    93	
    94	- C2, C6 and C7 lose some detail in this pilot. Examples: the "existential" wording in C2; the pinned-header rule
    95	  in C6; the missing-capture negative control in C7. The review must decide if each loss changes a meaning.
    96	- In section 2, "uses a true receipt again" renders "replays" and "reattributes". Check that this is the same meaning.
