   253	## A9.4 Revocation and historical security: not evaluated — AUTHOR DECISION B; OWNER DECISION for historical security. A weakening under A1 §A1.1.
   254	
   255	- **Weakened.** For V1 bundles, `VALID_STRICT` evaluates neither P6 condition: `key_authorized(declared_issue_time)`
   256	  against effective revocation, and no revocation effective at or before `max(anchor_time, declared_issue_time)`
   257	  (A4.3). Every report carries `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED` next to the verdict. *Why:* under the registered text
   258	  a verifier that cannot evaluate revocation returns `UNVERIFIABLE` for every receipt.
   259	- **Historical security.** Every report carries `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. V1 does not evaluate whether
   260	  a signing algorithm or key was safe when it signed. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply:
   261	  an authenticated safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared against T with A2.1's chain-time
   262	  uncertainty (CODEX-REVIEW 8). A surviving PQ attestation slot prevents a P-256 break alone from supplying every
   263	  required attestation signature. An ML-DSA-65 cutoff and the classical slot remain; historical authority, per-key
   264	  custody and continuing commitment and history security remain assumptions. A PQ slot never removes the need for
   265	  a theft cutoff.
   266	- **Issuer duty.** The V1 issuer never re-authorizes a key (R-5.24). V1 defines no revocation publication.
   267	- **Contract for later verifiers.** Missing status capture does not by itself invalidate a V1 receipt. A later
   268	  verifier given a V1 bundle, under the same governing V1 specification, policy, trust configuration and other
   269	  declared inputs, returns the same verdict and values in the stable field set (A9.1), and never a weaker verdict
   270	  because of missing status capture. **Verdict stability** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13, `docs/V1-V2-COMPATIBILITY.md`): a V1
   271	  bundle is always evaluated under its signed `common.spec`. Trust configurations are append-only (A9.5), so a
   272	  later configuration cannot turn a V1 verdict into `UNVERIFIABLE` by dropping a KSK, a header pin or a repository
   273	  key. Later algorithm policy enters the separate historical-security assessment only as an authenticated
   274	  per-algorithm cutoff compared with T, with A2.1's chain-time uncertainty; authenticated per-key theft cutoffs are
   275	  assessed separately there as well. It never overwrites the V1 verdict or its six stable fields, whether T is
   276	  before, equal to or after a cutoff. A receipt whose T precedes an algorithm's cutoff keeps its V1 verdict; that
   277	  comparison alone does not establish historical safety. Later revocation, standing, renewal and historical-security
   278	  results are reported separately, together with later adverse evidence. The V1 verdict continues to apply the
   279	  algorithm policy registered for its governing V1 specification, including its original malformed, unsupported and
   280	  prohibited-identifier cases (A9.7 row 29). This
   281	  binds later verifiers and profiles; it is not a property V1 itself demonstrates. **This is not
   282	  non-repudiation.** Evidence (C7): for an otherwise valid, fully performable bundle where missing status capture
   283	  is the sole difference, a companion returning `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` must go red.
   284	
   285	## A9.5 Strict-only, standalone; the receipt anchor — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12 except where marked
   286	
   287	- **Dispatch.** Out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch; A4.2 precedence holds inside.
   288	- **H1a's suite.** Forge, tamper, backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature, out-of-profile, the A9.1 mapping.
   289	  Degradation and wrapper instances move to their capabilities.
   290	- **Receipt anchor.** OTS stamps `SHA256(A_sig)`, tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`, which commits to both frames
   291	  and their exact detached signatures in manifest slot order (ENCODING-V1 §5, D-8, D-14). The verifier verifies the
   292	  committed signature bytes only. The frame-only layout is withdrawn, with no fallback.
   293	- **Issue order (D-15).** Fix manifest and metadata; build frames; sign; verify; **freeze** the signature bytes;
   294	  **stamp** `A_sig` (one stamp per receipt, R-3.42); obtain confirmation; ship. A signature made after T cannot fit
   295	  the anchor. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a fresh stamp. Signature identifiers stay independent
   296	  of signature bytes; the anchor digest may commit to them; nothing is normalized after stamping.
   297	- **Git stamp target (D-16).** The git channel's OTS stamp targets the **signed commit's object id**, never a tree,
   298	  blob or unsigned commit. The verifier recomputes the id, verifies `gpgsig`, follows commit→tree→blob, and checks
   299	  the stamped id. SHA-1 linkage remains a stated residual. The git evidence names its object format, and V1
   300	  accepts only `sha1`: the key-assertion repository stays in SHA-1 format at a host that supports it, whatever
   301	  git's default becomes (the author, 2026-10-13: Git 3 moves to SHA-256 in 2027; GitHub does not support it).
   302	  The pre-freeze bundle schema (V1-PROFILE §2.1, G-26) defines the git-evidence member `object_format` as the string
   303	  `"sha1"`. It is an evidence descriptor, not a new signed-frame or key-publication field. A missing descriptor gives
   304	  `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT_MISSING`; an unsupported value gives `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT`. The
   305	  verifier recomputes the SHA-1 object ids from the raw commit/tree/blob bytes, so this descriptor cannot override
   306	  their interpretation. For V1 the stamped message is exactly 40 lowercase ASCII hex digits naming the signed commit
   307	  id followed by one LF byte. The schema/check specification and conformance cases are fixed before the 30 Oct
   308	  profile freeze; no P8 frame or key-publication vector changes are required.
   309	- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose reliance
   310	  period contains that time validates it**. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance
   311	  lifetime L, with the reliance interval [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is the registered acceptance-policy
   312	  value derived from the cited root DNSKEY RRSIG validity evidence; a largest observed lifetime is labelled observed,
   313	  not asserted to be a proven global maximum. A candidate must also satisfy cryptographic validation and the selected
   314	  RRSIG's inception/expiration checks at the snapshot's own verified anchored time. This extension permits authentic
   315	  pre-retirement evidence anchored later; it does not establish that signing occurred before retirement. A thief of
   316	  a retired KSK can manufacture eligible root evidence during the extension. Exposure is bounded by the reliance
   317	  endpoint in snapshot chain time, not by a finite UTC duration under A2.1. Historical authority-key safety and the
   318	  continuing anchor/history assumptions remain necessary and are not evaluated by V1. **AUTHOR ADOPTED, 2026-10-13**
   319	  (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F2; kind CORRECT: the withdrawn cutoff claimed a protection DNSSEC itself does not
   320	  supply, since resolvers accept a retired KSK's signatures until they expire). The author: *"I'd argue that the
   321	  'narrowing of protection' wasn't a real narrowing, it was us admitting the real level of protection given the
   322	  constraints of the project."* The rule is existential and order-independent: a candidate that fails is
   323	  informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible key has been tried (F1). A trusted KSK that
   324	  is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure. The trust configuration records signing and
   325	  reliance periods separately from publication and revocation dates.
   326	- **Trust-configuration authority** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14, after `docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/` F04). The
   327	  trust configuration is authenticated independently of the bundle and of both authority-channel credentials. Each
   328	  verifier release compiles in the hash of its configuration. Releases are authenticated by a key that neither the
   329	  DNS credential, the repository key nor the issuing AWS account can use; the dated custody record names that key
   330	  and its access boundary. Capture of an authority-channel credential grants no ability to sign a release,
   331	  authorize a configuration hash, or replace the compiled trust root. CI is not the authenticity check. Compromise
   332	  of the release key is distribution compromise, a stated boundary.
   333	- **Trust configuration is append-only** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13). Trust configuration entries are append-only: KSKs, header
   334	  pins and repository keys are never removed, and finalized period endpoints never change. Repository trust entries
   335	  name D, the key and an inclusive signing interval; the git assertion passes this trust check if some configured
   336	  entry for D verifies gpgsig and its interval contains the signed commit's own verified anchored time. Repository
   337	  entries have no KSK-style reliance extension. Unknown material cannot establish this required check. Append-only
   338	  extensions preserve previously usable trust witnesses; newly covered evidence can improve an earlier UNVERIFIABLE
   339	  result. Exact stable-field identity remains the contract for identical declared inputs. Closing an open period must
   340	  not retrospectively exclude evidence previously accepted under that entry; the pre-freeze configuration records the
   341	  endpoint/update convention explicitly. **Rollover control:** a snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must pass
   342	  (SPIKE-REPORT, KSK-2024 re-capture; without it every receipt from 2026-10-11 would be falsely rejected).
   343	- **Custody.** V1 offers no renewal. Long-horizon validity depends on the dependency manifest and on the holder
   344	  keeping the exact bytes (a mirror that alters a signature byte breaks its copy, CODEX-REVIEW 5).
   345	
   346	## A9.6 Signers, the issuance window, and what they guarantee — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12
   347	
   348	**Signer profile, conditional.** The hybrid records the author's stated preference (2026-10-12: *"I think we
   349	should build our V1 with a PQ implementation"*). It is not a claim requirement: no sentence of C1–C7 needs it. It
   350	is the first item cut if 30 Oct slips (A9.2).
   351	- **If the hybrid spike passes by 21 Oct:** slot `kms` is AWS KMS **ML-DSA-65** (FIPS 204, in KMS's HSMs), raw
   352	  signature per ENCODING-V1 §5.2 rule R; slot `gpg` is **P-256** OpenPGP (rule O), signed by a
   353	  non-exportable, hardware-protected key in a separate technical key-custody domain outside the issuing AWS account
   354	  (*slot-2 custody*, below), pulling from a queue with no inbound path.
   355	  One pair algorithm identifier names both slot schemes (`common.alg` is shared, R-3.12). This adopts for V1 the
   356	  hybrid A3 §A3.6 deferred.
   357	- *Pass condition* (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-12, fixed after an offline pre-spike, `prototype/spike-pq/`, which the
   358	  author commits, and before the KMS spike):
   359	  - the pair identifier is registered with its mode and context;
   360	  - the mixed-format pairing passes R-3.12 and P3's model;
   361	  - a real KMS ML-DSA-65 signature over a V1 frame (external μ above 4 KB) verifies offline in two
   362	    implementations;
   363	  - a real P-256 signature from the `gpg` slot as deployed verifies offline;
   364	  - the full selected byte profile is specified and tested: attestation **and manifest-possession** verification
   365	    for both slots, slot key and fingerprint encodings, key-use assumptions appropriate to each slot (PROOF's
   366	    OpenPGP type-0x00 assumption does not describe the raw ML-DSA slot), and golden and rejection vectors with an
   367	    agreement run for rule R.
   368	
   369	  All of it by 21 Oct, or the fallback is taken automatically. The offline pre-spike is feasibility evidence only
   370	  (approximate JCS, no real KMS, a bare SLH-DSA portable slot rather than this pair); it does not satisfy the
   371	  condition.
   372	- **Registered fallback, otherwise:** both slots classical P-256 under P4': KMS-backed OpenPGP P-256, plus the
   373	  `gpg` slot under slot-2 custody. The signature-inclusive anchor and the
   374	  issuance window are retained unchanged.
   375	- **Slot-2 custody (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13).** The `gpg`-slot key is generated non-exportable in hardware outside
   376	  the issuing AWS account: a token on author-controlled hardware, or an HSM-backed key at a provider other than AWS.
   377	  The owner chooses between them, and records the choice, dated, in the profile before the 30 Oct freeze. The choice
   378	  takes into account measured token throughput and issuance availability, and the author's concern about scalability
   379	  (2026-10-13: *"Scalability is a real, legitimate, concern"*). Either choice satisfies the author's key-protection
   380	  requirement (A9.0) and leaves C1–C7 unchanged. The key's authority is evidenced by the key-publication record in
   381	  both cases.
   382	  - *Policy signer.* In either custody option, a queue-pulling policy signer outside the issuing AWS account
   383	    validates the complete V1 frame and enforces the declared-time check using its own clock before requesting a
   384	    signature. The issuing AWS account can enqueue frames but cannot invoke the slot-2 key directly, change that
   385	    policy or clock, or obtain the policy signer's key-use credentials. The slot-2 key is dedicated to V1 tagged
   386	    type-0x00 documents (attestation and manifest-possession). The dated custody record names this enforcement
   387	    boundary and its access policy. Non-exportability protects against key export, not signing misuse through the
   388	    permitted queue path.
   389	  - *Deadline.* For the hybrid, the final custody implementation must have completed the deployed-slot
   390	    pass-condition evidence by 21 Oct. The owner may defer selection until before 30 Oct only among custody
   391	    implementations that each met that deadline; otherwise the registered fallback is taken. A custody selection
   392	    changes neither rule O nor any claim, pass condition or exit obligation.
   393	  - *Limit.* Separate technical custody does not imply independent availability, independent signing fates,
   394	    separate control or independent approval of content; C3 applies to both options.
   395	- **Branch registration.** If the hybrid branch is taken, the owner registers, before the 30 Oct freeze, a dated
   396	  **branch-registration supplement** bounded to the pair identifier with its mode and context, the ENCODING-V1 §5.2
   397	  edit for rule R, and the completed pass-condition evidence, naming the exact ENCODING-V1 and specification
   398	  hashes. The signing-commit baseline plus that supplement governs the hybrid branch. This amendment authorizes
   399	  that supplement and nothing else in it; it needs no further amendment and no further author approval. Either
   400	  way, the owner records the branch taken, dated, in ENCODING-V1 §5.2 and the profile before the freeze.
   401	- **Declared-time check (issuer side, not a verifier rule).** The `gpg`-slot signer refuses a frame whose
   402	  `declared_issue_time` is outside ε (A9.2) of its own clock. Whoever controls the queue (an AWS takeover) still
   403	  has signing capability for both slots; the check limits that to minting at the current time. For an HSM-backed key,
   404	  this refusal is enforced by the policy signer, not by the HSM's generic signing API. Separation of control stays a
   405	  V2 driver (A9.3).
   406	
   407	**Issuance window.** The key-publication record carries `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after`, **both
   408	mandatory**, identical in DNS and the git blob (ENCODING-V1 §7, §7.1, D-17).
   409	- **Registered invariant: `issuance-not-after − issuance-not-before ≤ 90 days`** (a record that violates it is
   410	  rejected, `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`). This amendment registers the first issuance window only; it does not yet
   411	  authorize successor issuance. A future sequential-replacement registration may retain the frozen V1 byte/check
   412	  profile: the same DNS owner and git path, new keys, and a new window with next.not-before > previous.not-after (both
   413	  endpoints are inclusive). Each receipt carries its own historical snapshot and commit. Multiple TXT records at the
   414	  required owner fail `DNS_RRSET`; a substituted per-key owner name fails `DNS_OWNER`. The V1 verifier checks each
   415	  receipt's window, not non-overlap between receipts. Before successor issuance, the owner records a dated rotation
   416	  registration naming the two-window positive/negative vectors and the model, checked property and result
   417	  establishing the intended issuance invariant. This future rotation evidence is not added to the 30 Oct
   418	  first-window gate. Without that registration, issuance stops at the first window's end.
   419	- **Verifier rule (C4).** A receipt is `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` unless the declared issue time is not earlier
   420	  than not-before and the verified chain-time T is not later than not-after, with T as registered and no margin.
   421	  T itself is not compared with not-before.
   422	- **Issuer invariant.** An attempt whose T would exceed not-after is not shipped. The stop margin, the window's
   423	  actual dates and the rotation plan are operating numbers, not registered (`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md`).
   424	- A published window cannot be shortened; detection changes no V1 verdict.
   425	- The relying-party documentation states: `ISSUANCE_WINDOW` can result from presenting a later proof; ask the
   426	  holder for the issuer's original proof.
   427	
   428	**Exit.** The exit procedure is not part of this instrument; it is in the operations runbook. No V1 verifier rule
   429	consults any end-of-issuance statement (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-12).
   430	
   431	**The two product guarantees, stated conditionally (CODEX-REVIEW 7).** Guarantee 2 is the C4 window rule, stated
   432	as a property; a finding against it is a C4 finding for the closure rule (A9.2). Guarantee 1 is not computed.
   433	1. **Past receipts survive later compromise.** A receipt whose exact signatures are anchored by T cannot have
   434	   signatures manufactured after an adversary gains signing capability for every required slot substituted into
   435	   it, while SHA-256, the OTS commitment and the pinned chain history stay secure. Its historical authenticity also
   436	   needs the signing and key-use assumptions to have held before T, authentic historical authority, and no relevant
   437	   compromise before T; V1 does not evaluate these (`historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`). A later key theft or
   438	   algorithm break does not by itself invalidate such a receipt. It does not cure earlier theft, unauthorized
   439	   signing before T, misissuance or whole-author-sphere capture, and does not prove that the issuer itself obtained
   440	   the stamp or issued the bundle uniquely.
   441	2. **Signing capability mints only inside the window, in chain time.** Even signing capability for every required
   442	   slot cannot make a receipt pass V1 with verified T after its authenticated not-after, under continuing anchor
   443	   and history assumptions and provided the authority channels are not also captured (republishing a window is a
   444	   new authority event; whole capture is R-2.15). Translating this into a finite UTC cutoff requires an additional,
   445	   explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and
   446	   neither ε nor S is one. Inside the window, such an adversary mints receipts that pass; the window cannot be
   447	   shortened, and detecting misuse is the B(ii) add-on, not V1.
   448	
   449	**C3 non-claim.** The two keys are *separate technical failure domains under a single controller; no separation of
   450	control or independent fates.* The `gpg` signer signs frames delivered by the AWS-side issuer, so capture of the
   451	issuing account confers signing capability for both slots; the non-exportable key prevents key export, not misuse.
   452	With the hybrid the slots differ in algorithm (lattice and elliptic curve) and custody (AWS KMS and the
   453	slot-2 custody); under the fallback a P-256 break defeats both slots.
